    answer:
      "Distributed System Architecture:\nA distributed system is built in layers to hide complexity and simplify communication. Common layers are:\n• Physical Layer – Machines, network hardware, and cabling.\n• Network Layer – Protocols like TCP/IP that deliver messages.\n• Operating System Layer – Process management, memory, file system.\n• Middleware Layer – RPC, distributed objects, naming, transparency.\n• Application Layer – Distributed apps and services.\nKey components are clients, servers, network infrastructure, and middleware that hides the distributed nature of the system.\n\nMonolithic Kernel:\nA monolithic kernel places all OS services in kernel space (file system, memory, drivers, networking). Everything runs in one large binary and interacts via direct function calls.\nAdvantages: Fast, simple communication, mature.\nDisadvantages: Poor modularity, hard to maintain, one bug can crash entire system.\nExamples: Linux, traditional UNIX.\n\nMicrokernel:\nA microkernel keeps only essential OS parts in kernel space—mainly IPC, basic scheduling, and low-level memory management. Everything else (drivers, file systems, network stack) runs in user space as separate servers.\nAdvantages: Highly modular, reliable, easier to extend.\nDisadvantages: More context switching and IPC cost → slower.\nExamples: Mach, QNX, Minix.\n\nComparison:\n• Size: Monolithic large → Microkernel small.\n• Reliability: Microkernel better due to isolation.\n• Performance: Monolithic faster, microkernel slower due to message passing.\n• Flexibility: Microkernel more flexible/modular.",
