    answer:
      "Principles of RPC:\nRPC makes a remote function appear like a normal local procedure call. The client calls a function, and behind the scenes the client stub packs parameters, sends them across the network, the server stub unpacks and executes, and the result is returned.\nKey principles:\n• Transparency – Remote call looks local.\n• Stubs – Hide message passing details.\n• Marshalling – Convert arguments to a transferable format.\n• Request/Reply model – Client sends request, waits for reply.\n• Binding – Client locates the server through a binder service.\n\nDesign Issues of Distributed Systems:\n• Transparency – Hide location, migration, replication, and concurrency.\n• Reliability – Handle failures, use replication and failover.\n• Performance – Minimize communication, cache data, balance load.\n• Scalability – Avoid centralized components, use partitioning and replication.\n• Security – Authentication, authorization, encryption.\n• Heterogeneity – Different machines, OSes, and data formats must interoperate through standards and middleware.",
