      "Remote Procedure Call (RPC):\nRPC allows a program to call a procedure on a remote machine as if it were local. It hides network communication and lets programmers use normal function-call syntax.\nRPC works by: client stub → marshals args → sends request → server stub → executes procedure → marshals result → client receives reply.\n\nClient-Server Model:\nThe client sends requests for a service; the server processes and replies. Communication follows a request–reply pattern. Clients block until the server responds (in synchronous RPC).\n\nMarshaling:\nMarshaling converts parameters into a standardized byte format so they can travel across networks. This includes converting integers, strings, structures, and handling data issues like byte order. Unmarshaling reconstructs data at the receiver. Marshaling ensures different architectures can communicate correctly.",
